      "math_academy_id": "q-130679",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=-31.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{4}$ (switching the second and the fourth rows). What is the value of $det(B)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(-31) \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
